WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.320
Remember the echo from lesson two?

2
00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:05.460
You held the caller's clean voice
and knew the delay, two thousand samples,

3
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:07.380
so you subtracted a copy. Gone.

4
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.840
Then your line joins a partner
network across the sea,

5
00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:11.780
and callers hear every word twice.

6
00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.580
This time the echo arrives already
mixed in, with no clean copy.

7
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.340
And every route has its own delay.

8
00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:19.720
Your canceller has
two thousand samples baked in,

9
00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.860
so on a twelve hundred sample
echo it adds a second ghost.

10
00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.740
Can the recording itself
tell you the delay?

11
00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:28.910
Make the echo tiny:
four samples late, half as strong.

12
00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:31.390
Two thousand hertz comes out
one and a half times as tall.

13
00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.110
One thousand hertz
comes out at half height.

14
00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:34.870
Same echo, louder here, quieter there.

15
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:37.640
On the wheel, one thousand hertz turns

16
00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.100
forty-five degrees per sample.

17
00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.880
The echo sits four steps behind,

18
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:42.760
a half lap, and pulls against the tone.

19
00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.840
At two thousand hertz it sits
a full lap behind, right on top.

20
00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:48.260
Walk up through the tones,
and the gain rises and falls.

21
00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:49.080
That's a ripple.

22
00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:53.350
An echo D samples late ripples
every eight thousand over D hertz.

23
00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.570
Four samples,
it repeats every two thousand hertz.

24
00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.110
Forty samples, every two hundred.

25
00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:59.110
A longer delay makes a finer ripple.

26
00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:01.510
You never see the echo alone, though.

27
00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.490
The voice is loud near
seven hundred hertz,

28
00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.190
quiet near three thousand,
and the echo multiplies it.

29
00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.290
So the ripple swings by a hundred on
the hump, and by one in the valley.

30
00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:11.790
Take the log,
and multiplied turns into plus.

31
00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:13.750
Now it swings one point one everywhere.

32
00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.050
That's the log spectrum.

33
00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:17.130
Back to the tiny echo.

34
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:18.810
Its log gain repeats:

35
00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:22.730
point four one, point one one, minus
point six nine, point one one. How often?

36
00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:24.190
That's lesson twelve's question.

37
00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:25.430
Run the list through the FFT,

38
00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:26.750
and bin four lights up.

39
00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:27.730
Oh, and four is the delay.

40
00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:31.470
On a circle,
zero to eight thousand hertz is one lap,

41
00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.210
and the bottom half mirrors the top.

42
00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:37.790
Walk once round, and an echo D samples
late laps the tone D times: bin D.

43
00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:40.210
The FFT of the log
spectrum is the cepstrum.

44
00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:41.210
Spec, backwards.

45
00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.490
Its positions are quefrencies,
measured in time.

46
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:45.180
The ticket's call.

47
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.480
Take about one second of it.

48
00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.020
FFT, log, FFT.

49
00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.020
Skip below quefrency one hundred,
where the voice lives.

50
00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.120
The tallest peak: twelve hundred samples.

51
00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:54.160
Point one five seconds.

52
00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.520
One change from lesson two.

53
00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:57.600
There's no clean voice,

54
00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.560
so the canceller subtracts
a shifted copy of its own output.

55
00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.120
Set the delay to twelve hundred.

56
00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:03.780
Start the strength at twice the peak,

57
00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.940
point four,
and raise it until the peak is gone.

58
00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:07.720
The echo told you its own address.

59
00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:09.950
That's the last bug.

60
00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:11.570
One click, a wheel that never left,

61
00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:13.190
a row of test wheels on a keypad,

62
00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.050
and today, a spectrum of a spectrum.

63
00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:17.190
Next time a line echoes,
you'll know where to look.
